<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Havelberg</h1><p class="st-meta">948 CE – 1548 CE (approx.) · capital Wittstock</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A bishopric founded by Otto I among the Slavs of the Havel, lost in the revolt of 983 and refounded as a small territory that Brandenburg absorbed.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Otto I founded the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havelberg">Havelberg</a> in 946 or 948 at a stronghold on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a>, to convert the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-march">Northern March</a>. It became a suffragan of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/archbishopric-of-magdeburg">Archbishopric of Magdeburg</a> in 968. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-slav-rising">Great Slav Rising</a> of 983 destroyed it, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> lived in exile for more than a century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wendish-crusade">Wendish Crusade</a> of 1147, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anselm-of-havelberg">Anselm of Havelberg</a>, a noted theologian and diplomat to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>, restored the see, and the cathedral was consecrated in 1170. The bishops ruled a small territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havelberg">Havelberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittstock">Wittstock</a>, where they built their residence, but depended on the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, the last Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> died in 1548, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollern</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> installed their own family as administrators. The territory was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg</a> by 1598. The cathedral on the hill at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havelberg">Havelberg</a> survives.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-havelberg.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Havelberg" width="320" height="392"><figcaption>Image: Guido Radig · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Havelberg_an_der_Havel_Dom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>948 CE – 1548 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wittstock</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Anselm of Havelberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Low German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Havelberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1248&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-havelberg" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Havelberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4950 357 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Havelberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5270 613L5229 694M5270 613L5282 625"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5282 625h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 613h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 613h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1248&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-havelberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
